[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1mClassification: bounded.[0m The login flow already exists in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m with a single call site, so I'll present a short
  design in chat rather than write a spec. Say the word if you'd rather I go heavier.

  One thing blocks me before I can design it. [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m is called from exactly one place — the form
  submit handler — and nothing there has a user ID. The form collects only username and password, and [38;5;153mlogin[39m is a stub
  that returns [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username }[39m without talking to [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m. So "add a [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter" doesn't
  have an obvious source of data to fill it.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether [38;5;153muserId[39m is something the [3mcaller[0m supplies (a value that must be invented at the call site) or
  something the [3mlogin result[0m should carry (a value that only exists after the server responds).

  - [1mCaller generates a client-side ID[0m — [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, userId)[39m with the handler creating e.g.
    [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m. Buys you a per-attempt correlation ID that works today with no backend. Costs: it identifies
    the attempt, not the person, so it can't answer "who logged in" across sessions. Cheap to change later.
  - [1m[38;5;153muserId[39m is the account identifier from the backend[0m — then it belongs on the [3mreturn value[0m, not the parameter list; the
    signature stays [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m and the stub returns [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username, userId }[39m. Buys you
    the thing that actually tracks who logged in. Costs: the stub has to fake the ID until the real API call lands. This
    is the interface others would depend on, so it's the expensive one to get wrong.
  - [1m[38;5;153muserId[39m is just the username under another name[0m — add the parameter and pass [38;5;153musername[39m into it. Cheapest, but it's
    redundant with a field you already have.

  [1mMy recommendation: the second.[0m "Track who logged in" is a question about identity, and identity comes from the auth
  backend — a parameter the caller has to make up can't answer it. Adding the parameter now would bake a signature you'd
  have to unwind once the real API call exists.

